# Preliminary four-experiment run — 2026-08-11
# Context: node 1 = Pair A (mem0 e1:00.0 + mem1 c1:00.0, 2-way interleaved @256B,
#   two firmware windows dax0.0+dax1.0, ~508 GiB). Pair B (mem2 01:00.0 + mem3
#   02:00.0, decoder0.2, 512 GiB) = devdax dax2.0, held by root famfs FUSE server
#   since 2026-06-18. Tools: benchmarks/memory/cxlprobe.c (new, this date) and
#   benchmarks/memory/membench (same binary/params as 2026-05-19 smoke).
# CPUs: chase/victim pinned cpu 0+, aggressors cpu 16+ (all node-0 cores).

## E1 — stripe-trick validation (can software target one card of a pair?)
# Method: 2-way 256B interleave => stride=512,chunk=256,offset={0,256} should
# isolate one card if way = PA bit 8 (bit 8 is page-offset-controlled even at 4K).
full-seq  stream  node=1 threads=8  bw=47.49 GiB/s
classA    stride  node=1 threads=8 offset=0    bw=37.80 GiB/s   # > single-card max
classB    stride  node=1 threads=8 offset=256  bw=36.66 GiB/s   # > single-card max
co-run A+A (6T+6T, disjoint cores): 18.36 + 22.57 = 40.9 GiB/s total
co-run A+B (6T+6T, disjoint cores): 19.53 + 23.92 = 43.4 GiB/s total
# THP: never engaged (AnonHugePages=0; MADV_COLLAPSE rc=-1; thp=madvise mode).
# VERDICT: NEGATIVE. Both offset classes exceed one card's x8 ceiling and A+A
# co-run reaches ~full pair bandwidth => way-selection is NOT plain PA bit 8;
# AMD decoder hashes higher (page-dependent) address bits. Software CANNOT
# target an individual card inside a firmware-interleaved pair on Turin.
# Implication: BIOS de-interleave is the only path to per-card control.

## E2 — cross-pair interference (node 1 vs dax2.0) — BLOCKED
